		templ_7745c5c3_Err = templruntime.WriteString(templ_7745c5c3_Buffer, 14, "<article id=\"plastic\"><p>Not all plastics are recyclable, so the first step is to identify the types of plastic that can be recycled.  Look for the recycling symbol (a triangle of arrows) on the plastic item. Inside the symbol, you’ll see a number that indicates the type of plastic. Common recyclable plastics include; <b>PET (Polyethylene Terephthalate)</b> which is used in water bottles, soda bottles, and food containers and <b>HDPE (High-Density Polyethylene)</b> which can be found in detergent bottles, milk jugs, and shampoo containers. Plastics like PVC (used in pipes) or polystyrene (Styrofoam) are harder to recycle, so check with your local recycling center or platform like Bel Tech for guidelines.</p><p>One of the most important steps in plastic recycling is to clean the items before sending them for recycling. Dirty plastic can contaminate entire batches of recyclables, making it harder to process. This is how to clean plastics properly:<div class=\"my-4 flex flex-col gap-1 items-start text-justify\"><h6>- Rinse out food containers, bottles, and jars to remove any residue.</h6><h6>- Use plain water and avoid leaving food scraps or liquids inside.</h6><h6>- For sticky residues, use a little soap to clean thoroughly.</h6></div>For instance, before recycling a yoghurt or coke container, rinse it out completely and remove any attached foil or paper labels.</p><p>Many plastic items come with non-plastic materials like metal caps, paper labels, or rubber seals. These need to be removed before recycling.  Recycling facilities are designed to process pure plastic, so mixing materials can cause problems. You can separately recycle the non-plastic parts if possible, or dispose of them properly.</p><p>To save space and make transportation easier, flatten or compress plastic items before recycling. This is especially useful for bulky plastic bottles or containers. Here’s how to do it:<div class=\"my-4 flex flex-col gap-1 items-start text-justify\"><h6>- Crush plastic bottles after removing the caps.</h6><h6>- Stack flat items like trays or containers into a sack to save space.</h6></div>Flattening also ensures that more plastic can fit into collection bins or bags, making the process more efficient.</p><p>To keep plastics in perfect condition for recycling, store them in a clean, dry place before dropping them off at a recycling center or collection point.  This helps prevent contamination by dirt or moisture, which can make the plastic harder to process. For instance, you can use a separate bag or container specifically for recyclable plastics. Also avoid mixing plastics with other waste like food scraps or glass. In Nigeria, platforms like Bel Tech provide designated collection points for plastics. You can drop off your cleaned and sorted plastics at these points to ensure they are properly recycled.</p></article>")
